## Issue / fix: feat: Add support for Android build variants (feat #14777)

## Description
# Problem

When using `--config` to override the `identifier` field for Android dev/build commands ([per Tauri documentation](https://v2.tauri.app/develop/configuration-files/#extending-the-configuration)), Tauri fails with a "Project directory does not exist" error. The same approach works correctly for iOS, where the bundle identifier is successfully overridden at runtime.

This prevents a common development workflow: having both debug and release versions of an app installed side-by-side on the same device
For example, a developer may need to test a debug build while also having the App Store release version installed.
This is especially important when testing device-specific issues, or crashes that occur only in either debug or release, but not the other, such as bugs related to code obfuscation for release builds.

# Resolution
We have added the `applicationIdSuffix` to the `build.gradle.kts` file when we init the Android project, and default the value to `.debug`.
This matches to what happens when a new Android project is created in Android Studio, and the approach described in the Android docs for [configuring build variants](https://developer.android.com/build/build-variants#build-types).

With these changes, when the user runs `tauri android dev`, the debug build variant will be run.
In practice, this creates an app with the identifier `[base identifier][applicationIdSuffix]`, such as `com.owner.exampleapp.debug`.

